ifferential/14071891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "0b06589f536a568a60fc1910670924fbac977e5ba5ad6e964bdb58232c0b32b8" "basis" 2600 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "0b06589f536a568a60fc1910670924fbac977e5ba5ad6e964bdb58232c0b32b8" "basis" 2600 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "None"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "0b06589f536a568a60fc1910670924fbac977e5ba5ad6e964bdb58232c0b32b8" "basis" 2600 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "0b06589f536a568a60fc1910670924fbac977e5ba5ad6e964bdb58232c0b32b8" "basis" 2600 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-335247" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-335247" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\sqrt{x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+t}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R$ (since $x+t\\ge c>0$ on $R$). Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{x({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2x{t}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t>1$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ never vanishes on $R,$ so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-335247" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6b573410e546e85737b05b06e8af7ecb7c8137d3fe526b0da77751eac4af1b2"} {"question" "q-335247" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-335247" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-335247" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-{t}^{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 0$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{-{t}^{2}x}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=-2tx{e}^{-{t}^{2}x},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6b573410e546e85737b05b06e8af7ecb7c8137d3fe526b0da77751eac4af1b2"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-350548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+{t}^{5})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-350548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+{t}^{5})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(4x+{t}^{5})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(4x+{t}^{5})^{2}] & =2(4x+{t}^{5})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(4x+{t}^{5}) \\\\  & =2(4x+{t}^{5})\\cdot 5{t}^{4} \\\\  & =10{t}^{4}(4x+{t}^{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}10{t}^{4}(4x+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10{t}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{t}^{5}x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2+{t}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=10{t}^{4}(2+{t}^{5}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-350548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6b573410e546e85737b05b06e8af7ecb7c8137d3fe526b0da77751eac4af1b2"} {"question" "q-350548" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-350548" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "20t^4+10t^9"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-350550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-350550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})^{2}] & =2({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\cdot 4{t}^{3} \\\\  & =8{t}^{3}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}8{t}^{3}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{t}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{0}^{2}+{t}^{4}x{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}+2{t}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=8{t}^{3}(\\frac{8}{3}+2{t}^{4}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-350550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6b573410e546e85737b05b06e8af7ecb7c8137d3fe526b0da77751eac4af1b2"} {"question" "q-350550" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-350550" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{64}{3}t^3+16t^7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-350551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-350551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}] & =2(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5}) \\\\  & =2(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\cdot 5{t}^{4} \\\\  & =10{t}^{4}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{4}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10{t}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{t}^{5}x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-1)+({t}^{5}-(-{t}^{5})) \\\\  & =2{t}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=10{t}^{4}(2{t}^{5})=20{t}^{9}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-350551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6b573410e546e85737b05b06e8af7ecb7c8137d3fe526b0da77751eac4af1b2"} {"question" "q-350551" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"} {"question" "q-350551" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "20t^9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073413/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7bcab6a87eef0a90f41db4956e9be38bd29b904745222180d3015e17c4f2b43"}] "usage" {"q-335252" [] "q-335247" []} "basis" 2694 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186325536 17592186325537 17592186325538 17592186325539 17592186325540 17592186374081 17592186374082 17592186374083 17592186374084 17592186374085] "immutable_fields" [[17592186325535 :answer-field/correct] [17592186325535 :answer-field/id] [17592186325535 :answer-field/key] [17592186325535 :answer-field/type] [17592186374080